          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How do I know whether a three-character category can stand on its own as a usable code?",
          "answer": "A three-character ICD-10-CM category is usable on its own only when the classification has no further subdivisions for it. The sources here show this is the exception, not the rule.\n\n**The general rule.** The coding structure summary says the three-character form is the category, and that characters four and five add detail such as cause, site, or severity. It also says most categories need a fourth, fifth, or sixth character before the code is billable. (*ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*)\n\n**The exception, hypertension.** I10, essential (primary) hypertension, is a complete code at three characters. The hypertension summary notes that most categories need more characters, but I10 takes none. It also says a request to add specificity to I10 cannot be satisfied by the classification. (*Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*)\n\n**Categories that never stand alone.** The asthma summary says J45 is never used at three characters. Severity and current state of the disease fill the remaining characters. (*Coding asthma: J45 and its severity and exacerbation axes*) The diabetes summary says E11 has its own subcategories by cause and type, but it does not say E11 itself is unusable. I could not confirm from these passages whether E11 alone is billable, so check that directly.\n\n**A practical test.** Look up the category in the official ICD-10-CM tables. If it has child codes beneath it, it needs more characters. If it has no children, the three-character form is the full code. The passages I retrieved do not describe that lookup step in detail, so I cannot give you a full procedure from them.\n\nFor coding decisions in a real claim or record, confirm with a certified medical coder or the current official code set.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
